% ---- Page-spanning continuation markers (layout standard C9/C10/C11) ----
% When a breakable styled box (code listing or callout) splits across a page,
% signpost the span the way a continued longtable does: a "continues on next
% page" marker in the FOOTER on the part where it breaks, and a "continued from
% previous page" marker in the HEADER on the part where it resumes. The
% breakable library keeps the colored frame/fill intact on every part by itself;
% these overlays only add the wording. The overlay keys target exactly the right
% parts -- "first and middle" = every part that is followed by more (gets the
% footer), "middle and last" = every part that follows more (gets the header) --
% so an UNBROKEN box shows no markers at all. Drawn just outside the frame edge
% so they never overprint the box content.
\tcbset{
  contmarkers/.style={
    enhanced,
    overlay first and middle={%
      \node[anchor=north east, font=\scriptsize\itshape, text=black!55, inner sep=0pt]
        at ([yshift=-2.5pt]frame.south east) {continues on next page $\rightarrow$};%
    },
    overlay middle and last={%
      \node[anchor=south east, font=\scriptsize\itshape, text=black!55, inner sep=0pt]
        at ([yshift=2.5pt]frame.north east) {$\hookrightarrow$ continued from previous page};%
    },
  }
}

% Shared geometry for all five code blocks (matches the live publications):
